107 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1693 0 R/A 1692 0 R/Alt(A dartboard with five concentric circles labeled 1 to 5, from outer to innermost, has a radius of r. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
108 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1695 0 R/A 1694 0 R/Alt(A histogram of the probability distribution of averages of samples.\n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis ranges from 0 to 12, in increments of 2. The vertical axis measures the averages of samples and ranges from 0 to 0.14, in increments of 0.02. A histogram has multiple bars from values 2 to 12. The bar at 7.3 is the highest and the bars at 2 and 12 are the lowest. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
109 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1697 0 R/A 1696 0 R/Alt(The function, f subscript x of x is graphed. F subscript x is discontinuous, and makes several jumps at certain values of x. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0.0 to 3.5, in increments of 0.5. The vertical axis measures f subscript x of x and ranges from 0.000 to 1.000, in increments of 0.250. A data point at \(0.0, 0.125\) has a horizontal line ending at \(1.0, 0.125\).  A data point at \(1.0, 0.500\) has a horizontal line ending at \(2.0, 0.500\).  A data point at \(2.0, 0.875\) has a horizontal line ending at \(3.0, 0.875\).  A data point at \(3.0, 1.000\) has a horizontal line ending at \(3.3, 1.000\).  All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
110 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1699 0 R/A 1698 0 R/Alt(The function f subscript x of x is graphed. F subscript x is discontinuous and makes several jumps at certain values of x. \n\n"\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x, and ranges from 0 to 16 in increments of 1. The vertical axis measures f subscript x of x and ranges from 0.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.1. 15 data points on the plot follow a concave down, increasing trend. A data point starting at \(1, 0.3\) has a horizontal line ending at \(2, 0.3\).  A data point starting at \(3, 0.7\) has a horizontal line ending at \(4.5, 0.7\). A data point starting at \(15, 0.95\) has a horizontal line ending at \(16, 0.95\).  All data are approximate. \n"\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
111 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1701 0 R/A 1700 0 R/Alt(A normal distribution and the interval probabilities corresponding to P, open parenthesis, a less than or equal to X less than or equals to b, close parenthesis equals F subscript X of b minus F subscript X of a, is represented as a shaded area under the curve. \n\n"\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from negative 4 to 4 in increments of 1.  A bell-shaped curve that peaks at x equals 0 is labeled f subscript x of x. The area under the curve, on the right, where x equals 1.3 and 2.5 is shaded. The ends of the shaded area are labeled a and b. The shaded area is labeled P, open parenthesis, a less than or equal to X less than or equal to b, close parenthesis, equals F subscript X of b minus F subscript X of a. All data are approximate. \n"\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
112 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1703 0 R/A 1702 0 R/Alt(The transformation equation y equals sine squared of x is represented as two symmetrical bell curves. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and has points labeled pi and 2 pi.  The vertical axis measures sine squared of x and ranges from 0.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.2. A bell-shaped curve originating at the origin peaks and drops to \(pi, 0\). Another bell-shaped curve continues from \(pi, 0\), peaks, and drops to \(2 pi, 0\). A horizontal line representing y at \(0, 0.58\) extends toward the right. The x coordinates of the curves, where they intersect with the horizontal line y, are  x 1, x 2, x 3, and x 4. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
113 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1705 0 R/A 1704 0 R/Alt(A strictly increasing F of x and a non-decreasing F of x graphs are graphed.\n\n"\n\nIn the graphs, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.2.  The vertical axis measures F of x and ranges from 0.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.2. Strictly increasing F of x: A curve beginning at the origin becomes convex, with an increasing trend to reach \(0.9, 1.0\) and inflects. A point labeled y is indicated at \(0, 0.3\). A dotted line from point y extends toward the curve, and the x coordinate of the point, 0.5 is indicated. Nondecreasing F of x: A curve beginning at the origin becomes convex with an increasing trend to reach \(0.3, 0.3\), stabilizes for a short distance, and then increases from \(0.5, 0.3\) to reach \(0.9, 1.0\) and inflects. A point labeled y is indicated at \(0, 0.3\). A dotted line from the point y extends toward the curve and the x coordinates of the point, 0.3 and  0.5 are indicated as x 1 and x 2 respectively. \n"\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
114 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1707 0 R/A 1706 0 R/Alt(Exponential distributions corresponding to different lambda values, such as one-fifth, one-third, and 1, are graphed. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0.0 to 3.0 in increments of 0.5. The vertical axis measures f of x and ranges from 0 to 5 in increments of 1. Lambda equals one-fifth: The curve drops from \(0, 5\) to \(0.5, 0.5\) and \(1.5, 0\). Lambda equals one-third: The curve drops from \(0, 3\) to \(0.4, 0.5\) and \(1, 0\). Lambda equals one: The curve drops from \(0, 1\) to \(1, 1.5\), and \(3,0\). All data are approximate.  \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
115 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1709 0 R/A 1708 0 R/Alt(Two p d fs corresponding to f 1 of x equals 1 divided by the square root of 2 pi x times e raised to the power negative log x squared over 2 and f 2 of x equals f 1 of x times, open parenthesis, 1 plus sine of 2 pi log x, close parenthesis.\n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 5 in increments of 1. The vertical axis x ranges from 0.0 to 1.4 in increments of 0.2. F 1 of x: A curve originating at the origin increases to \(0.4, 0.6\) and drops gradually to \(3, 0.1\) and \(0, 5\). F 2 of x: A curve originating at the origin peaks at \(0.2, 1.0\), \(0.5, 1.3\), and \(1.5, 0.6\) and drops to \(2,0\). It gradually increases to \(3, 0.1\) and drops to \(5,0\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
116 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1711 0 R/A 1710 0 R/Alt(The Poisson approximation, along with a binominal mass function, is graphed.\n\nThe horizontal axis measures x with values ranging from 0 to 12 in increments of 2. The vertical axis measures f of x with values ranging from 0.0 to 0.3 in increments of 0.1. The binomial mass function is represented as solid lines and Poisson approximation is indicated as dotted lines next to the solid lines. A series of bell-shaped vertical lines are indicated from values 1 to 11. The first four sets of lines are of increasing height and the following 7 pairs of lines are of decreasing height. A solid line extending from \(4, 0\) to \(4, 0.22\) and a dotted line extending from \(4.1, 0\) to \(4.1, 0.8\) are the highest. A solid line extending from \(11, 0\) to \(11, 0.01\) and \(10, 0\) to \(10, 0.01\) and a dotted line extending from \(10, 0\) to \(10.1, 0.01\) are the lowest.  \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
117 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1713 0 R/A 1712 0 R/Alt(A bell-shaped, symmetrical curve extends from negative 4 to 4 along the horizontal axis labeled x. It peaks at x equals 0. Dotted lines extend from negative 2, negative 1, 0, 1, and 2 toward the curve. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
118 0 obj<</K 1/Pg 1713 0 R/A 1714 0 R/Alt(A bell-shaped, symmetrical curve extends from values 7 to 23 along the horizontal axis. It peaks at the horizontal axis value of 15. 15 tightly packed, vertical bars of equal width extend toward the edges of the curve.  \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
119 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1716 0 R/A 1715 0 R/Alt(Three curves of different shapes representing the beta densities are shown.\n\nThe horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.2. The vertical axis ranges from 0 to 6 in increments of 2.  A curve originating at \(0,1\) and ending at \(1, 0.6\) has two peaks at \(0.1, 4.6\) and \(0.9, 4.6\) and a trough between the peaks at \(0.5, 0\).  A curve originating at \(0,0\) gradually increases to a peak at \(0.2, 2.6\) and falls to the lowest value of \(0.7, 0\). Then it increases to reach \(0.98, 6\). A curve originating at \(0.08, 6\), drops to \(0.2, 0\) and increases to \(0.78, 2.6\). Then it drops to \(1, 0\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
120 0 obj<</K 1/Pg 1716 0 R/A 1717 0 R/Alt(Nine different curves represent the symmetric beta densities when graphed.\n\nThe horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.2. The vertical axis ranges from 0 to 6 in increments of 2.  Four curves originating at \(0,6\) follow a convex, decreasing trend and fall sharply to nearly \(0.1, 1\) and remain stable. These curves then follow a convex, increasing trend and increase rapidly from \(0.9, 1\) to \(1, 6\). Five bell-shaped curves with different peaks extend between 0 and 1. The wider and shorter curve peaks at \(0.5, 2\), and the narrower and taller peaks at \(0.5, 5.8\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
121 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1719 0 R/A 1718 0 R/Alt(Two normal distribution curves extending between negative 4 and 4. The horizontal axis measures x and ranges from negative 4 to 4 in increments of 1. A bell-shaped curve labeled normal is narrower and taller. A bell-shaped curve labeled Cauchy is wider and shorter compared to the one labeled normal. Both curves peak at x equals 0. \n\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
122 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1721 0 R/A 1720 0 R/Alt(Examples of lognormal densities and gamma densities are depicted in two graphs. \n\nIn the graphs, the horizontal axis ranges from 0 to 12 in increments of 4. The vertical axis ranges from 0.0 to 0.8 in increments of 0.2. Lognormal densities: Five right-skewed distributions and one bell-shaped curve are depicted. A right-skewed curve with the highest peak rises sharply from the origin, peaks at \(1, 0.74\), drops to \(3,0\), and trails away. The bell-shaped curve extends between 6 and 12 and peaks at \(10, 0.4\). Gamma densities: Seven curves of different shapes are depicted. Two convex, decreasing, four right-skewed distributions, and a bell-shaped curve are depicted.  The bell-shaped curve extends between 4 and 12 and peaks at \(8, 0.3\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
123 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1723 0 R/A 1722 0 R/Alt(Two normal distribution curves of the same height. The horizontal axis measures x and ranges from negative 4 to 6 in increments of 1. A bell-shaped curve extending between negative 4 and 4 peaks at x equals 0. Another bell-shaped curve extending between negative 2 and 6 peaks at x equals 2. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
124 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1725 0 R/A 1724 0 R/Alt(An exponential distribution plot with graphs for various values of mu.\n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x with values ranging from 0 to 10 in increments of 1. Four tick marks at equal distances are indicated on the vertical axis.  Five curves originating away from the origin on the vertical axis at different horizontal axis values such as 0, 1, 2.1, 3.8, and 4.5 follow a concave, decreasing trend to reach \(10,0\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
125 0 obj<</K 1/Pg 1725 0 R/A 1726 0 R/Alt(Five bell curves of different sizes depict the members of the same scale family. The horizontal axis measures x and ranges from negative 5 to 6 in increments of 1. All five bell curves extending from negative 6 to 6, peak at x equals 0. The wider curve has the shortest peak while the narrow curve has the tallest peak. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
126 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1728 0 R/A 1727 0 R/Alt(Four bell curves depict the members of the same location-scale family. \n\nThe horizontal axis measures x and ranges from negative 6 to 6 in increments of 1. A vertical line extends from x equals 0. A curve originating at negative 5.6 peaks at negative 2 and drops to 0.4. A curve originating at negative 6, peaks at 0, and drops to 6. A curve originating at negative 5.6 peaks at negative 1 and drops to 3. A curve originating at negative 5.6 peaks at 1.8 and drops to 5. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
127 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1730 0 R/A 1729 0 R/Alt(A graph with three intersecting lines, x equals y, x plus y equals 1 and x equals 0 shows two shaded areas. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 2 in increments of 1. The vertical axis measures y and ranges from 0 to 2 in increments of 1. A line with a negative slope, labeled x plus y equals 1, extends from \(0,1\) to \(1,0\). A line with a positive slope, labeled x equals y extends from \(0,0\) to \(2,2\). The line intersects at \(0.5, 0.5\). A triangular area bounded by the lines x equals y, x equals 0, and x plus y equals 1 is shaded dark. A larger polygonal area with three sides covered by the lines x equals 0, x equals y, and x plus y equals 1 is shaded with a light color. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
128 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1732 0 R/A 1731 0 R/Alt(Two areas where f of x, y is greater than 0 are indicated in two graphs. The relationship between x and y is represented as linear.\n\nIn the graphs, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 1 in increments of 0.5. The vertical axis measures y and ranges from 0 to 2 in increments of 1. Graph A: A diagonal line extends from \(0,0\) to \(1, 1\). A diagonal line that is parallel to the aforementioned line extends from \(0,1\) to \(1,2\). The area between the two lines is shaded. Graph B: A diagonal line extends from \(0,0\) to \(1, 1\). A diagonal line that is parallel to the aforementioned line extends from \(0,0.1\) to \(1,1.1\). The area between the two lines is shaded. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
129 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1734 0 R/A 1733 0 R/Alt(The area where f of x, y is greater than 0 is indicated. The relationship between x and y is represented as a parabola. \n\nIn the graphs, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from negative 1.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.5. The vertical axis measures y and ranges from 0 to 1.5 in increments of 0.5. A curve beginning at \(negative 1, 1\) gradually drops to \(0,0\) and increases to \(1,1\). A curve beginning at \(negative 1, 1.1\) gradually decreases to \(0, 0.1\) and increases to \(1, 1.1\). The area between the curves is shaded. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
130 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1736 0 R/A 1735 0 R/Alt(A convex function lying above two tangent lines and the distance between the tangent lines.\n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 2 in increments of 1. The vertical axis ranges from 0 to 2 in increments of 1. A curve originating at \(0,1\) gradually declines to \(1, 0.6\) and increases to reach \(2,2\). A line with a negative slope extends from \(0, 0.9\) to \(1.1, 0.1\). A line with a positive slope extends from \(1, 0.5\) to \(2, 1.7\). Both the lines touch the curve at points x 1 and x 2 which are 0.5 and 1.5 respectively. The distance between x 1 and x 2 is shown as lambda g of x 1 plus, open parenthesis, 1 minus lambda, close parenthesis,  g of x 2. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
131 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1738 0 R/A 1737 0 R/Alt(The function g of x lying above a tangent line l of x equals a plus b x is graphed.\n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x, and the midpoint is labeled E of x. The curve g of x, originating almost at the midpoint of the vertical axis gradually declines to a certain distance and increases. A line with a positive slope, labeled l of x equals a plus b x, extends below the curve touching the curve at point E of x. A vertical line extends from point E of x to the curve. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
132 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1740 0 R/A 1739 0 R/Alt(Two lines corresponding to v equals a minus r divided by 2 and v equals r divided by 2 create a triangular region. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures r, and a point a is indicated on the far end of the horizontal axis. The vertical axis measures v. The points a and a divided by 2 are indicated on the vertical axis.  A line with a negative slope, labeled v equals a minus r divided by 2, extends from \(0, a\) to \(a, a divided by 2\). A line with a positive slope, labeled v equals r divided by 2, extends from \(0, 0\) to \(a, a divided by 2\). The area between the lines is shaded. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
133 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1742 0 R/A 1741 0 R/Alt(A histogram overlaid with a concave, decreasing curve is shown. \n\n"\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures y and ranges from 0.0 to 9.0 in increments of 1.0. The vertical axis represents exponential p d f and ranges from 0.0 to 0.5 in increments of 0.1. A histogram with nearly 20 bars from values 0.0 to 9.0. The bars decrease in height from left to right.  The bar at 0.0 is the highest and the bar at 10.0 is the lowest. A curve originating at \(0.0, 0.5\), follows a concave, decreasing trends to reach \(3.0, 0.1\) and \(9.0, 0.0\). All data are approximate. \n"\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
134 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1744 0 R/A 1743 0 R/Alt(A right-skewed histogram with multiple bars for Poisson sample variance compared against relative frequency. The bars are between Poisson sample variance of 5 and 45. The bars from the left are of increasing heights, with the highest bar at a Poisson sample variance 15 and a relative frequency of 0.06. The bars following the highest bar are of decreasing height. The lowest bars are at a Poisson sample variance of 2 and 46 where relative frequency is 0. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
135 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1746 0 R/A 1745 0 R/Alt(A beta distribution shows a slightly right-skewed distribution extending between 0.0 and 0.8. The horizontal axis is labeled v with values ranging from 0.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.2. The vertical axis is labeled u with values ranging from 0.0 to 3.0 in increments of 0.5. A curve originating at \(0,0\) peaks at \(0.3, 2.6\) and drops to \(0.8, 0\). A horizontal line at the peak is labeled, maximum equals 2.669. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
136 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1748 0 R/A 1747 0 R/Alt(A comparison of M S E of x bar and M S E of P hat B for two sample sizes, 4 and 400. \n\nIn the graphs, the horizontal axis measures P and ranges between 0.0 and 1.0 in increments of 0.5. M S E of X bar for n equals 4: The vertical axis ranges from 0.000 to 0.100 in increments of 0.025. An inverted U-shaped curve originating at \(0,0\) increases gradually to peak at \(0.5, 0.0625\). Then it decreases to reach \(1, 0\). M S E of P hat B for n equals 4: A horizontal line extends from 0.03 toward the right. M S E of X bar for n equals 400: The vertical axis ranges from 0.00000 to 0.00100 in increments of 0.00025. An inverted U-shaped curve originating at \(0,0\) increases gradually to peak at \(0.5, 0.000625\). Then it decreases to reach \(1, 0\). M S E of P hat B for n equals 400: A horizontal line extends from 0.00060 toward the right. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
137 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1750 0 R/A 1749 0 R/Alt(The risk function of three variance estimators show concave, increasing curves. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures sigma squared and ranges from 0 to 10 in increments of 2. The vertical axis measures the risk and ranges from 0 to 40 in increments of 10. A curve labeled R of sigma squared, S squared originating at \(0,0\), increases to reach \(9, 40\). A dotted curve labeled R of sigma squared, sigma cap squared originating at \(0,0\), increases to reach \(9, 30\). A dotted curve labeled R of sigma squared, S tilde squared originating at \(0,0\), increases to reach \(9, 25\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
138 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1752 0 R/A 1751 0 R/Alt(A concave, decreasing curve representing lambda of x is depicted. In the graph, the horizontal axis is labeled with the points 0, theta subscript 0, theta subscript 0 plus 2 n, and theta subscript 0 plus 4 n. The vertical axis ranges from 0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.5. A vertical line extends from theta subscript 0 to 1.0 on the vertical axis. A curve originating at \(theta subscript 0, 1.0\), decreases gradually to reach \(theta subscript 0 plus 4 n, 0\). All data are approximate.  \n\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
139 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1754 0 R/A 1753 0 R/Alt(Two concave, increasing curves, labeled beta 1 of theta and beta 2 of theta, represent the power functions.\n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures theta and ranges from 0.00 to 1.00 in increments of 0.25. The vertical axis ranges from 0.00 to 1.00 in increments of 0.25. A curve originating at \(0.08, 0\) increases to \(0.75, 1.00\) and inflects to reach \(1.00, 1.00\). A curve originating at \(0.25, 0\) increases to \(1.00, 1.00\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
140 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1756 0 R/A 1755 0 R/Alt(The values of beta of theta is represented as an S-shaped curve.\n\nThe horizontal axis measures theta and is labeled with the values 0, theta subscript 0, and theta subscript 0 plus 2 sigma over the square root of n. The vertical axis ranges from 0.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.5. An S-shaped curve labeled beta of theta originates close to the origin and increases to \(theta subscript 0 plus 2 sigma over the square root of n, 1.0\) and inflects and remains stable. The area below the curve, that is left to theta subscript 0 is shaded. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
141 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1758 0 R/A 1757 0 R/Alt(The power functions of three tests are represented by the curves beta 1 of theta, beta 2 of theta, and beta 3 of theta. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures theta. The values theta subscript 0 and theta subscript 2 are labeled on the horizontal axis. To the left of theta subscript 0 is theta subscript 0 minus 2 sigma over the square root of n. To the right of theta subscript 0 is theta subscript 0 plus 2 sigma over the square root of n. Beta 1 of theta: The curve originating from the left of the vertical axis follows a concave up, decreasing trend to reach a point close to theta subscript 0, on the right. Beta 2 of theta: The curve originating from the left of the vertical axis, close to theta subscript 0, follows a concave up, increasing trend. Beta 3 of theta: A U-shaped curve from the left of the vertical axis to the right of the vertical axis. All three curves intersect at a point close to the origin on the vertical axis. The area below the point of intersection of the three curves is shaded. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
142 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1760 0 R/A 1759 0 R/Alt(The risk function of a U M P test is represented by two curves. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures z with values ranging from negative 3 to 3 in increments of 1. The vertical axis measures R, open parenthesis, theta, delta, close parenthesis, with values ranging from 0 to 3 increments of 1. A concave up, increasing curve extends from \(0, negative 3\) to \(0, 3\). A concave up, decreasing curve extends from \(0, 0.8\) to \(1.7, 0\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
143 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1762 0 R/A 1761 0 R/Alt(The relationship between confidence intervals and acceptance areas is depicted using two diagonal, parallel lines, the upper and lower. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures mu and the vertical axis measures x bar. The upper line is a diagonal line that intersects with the vertical axis and extends toward the top right. The lower line is parallel to the upper line and intersects with the horizontal axis. The x-intercepts of the upper and lower lines away from the origin are indicated and the distance between them is indicated as c of x bar asterisk.  The y-intercepts of the upper and lower lines away from the origin are indicated as x dash asterisk. A point mu 0 is indicated close to the origin on the horizontal axis. The y-intercept of the upper and lower lines for the point mu 0 are indicated, and the distance between the are labeled A of mu 0. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
144 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1764 0 R/A 1763 0 R/Alt(The acceptance area and confidence interval for lambda 0 are graphed.\n\n"Acceptance region:  In the graph, the horizontal axis measures sigma X i. A parabola originating close to origin, opens downward. A point k asterisk is labeled close to the origin on the vertical axis. The x-intercepts of the k asterisk of the curve are indicated and the distance between them is labeled A of lambda 0. \nConfidence intervals: In the graph, the horizontal axis measures lambda. A right-skewed distribution originating close to the origin, peaks and trails away. A point k asterisk is labeled close to the origin on the vertical axis. The x-intercepts of the k asterisk of the curve are indicated and the distance between them is labeled C of x. \n"\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
145 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1766 0 R/A 1765 0 R/Alt(The difference between the 90 percent credible intervals and 90 percent confidence intervals are graphed.\n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures lambda and ranges from 0 to 1.2 in increments of 0.2. The vertical axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 8 in increments of 2. 9 sets of credible and confidence intervals are indicated in the plane. The credible intervals are shorter than confidence intervals and their upper endpoints are closer to the origin compared to that of the confidence intervals. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
146 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1768 0 R/A 1767 0 R/Alt(The credible probabilities of the 90 percent credible intervals and 90 percent confidence intervals are represented by a horizontal line and a steadily decreasing line, respectively. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 50 in increments of 10. The vertical axis measures the credible probabilities and ranges from 0 to 1 in increments of 0.02.  The credible intervals show a stable, horizontal  line from \(0, 0.9\), extending toward the right. The confidence intervals follow a decreasing trend. It passes through the points \(2, 0.96\), \(30, 0.9\), and \(50, 0.87\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
147 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1770 0 R/A 1769 0 R/Alt(The coverage probabilities of the 90 percent credible intervals and 90 percent confidence intervals. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures lambda and ranges from 0 to 3 in increments of 0.5. The vertical axis measures coverage probabilities and ranges from 0 to 1 in increments of 0.05.  A horizontal line extends from 0.9 on the vertical axis to the right. The credible intervals form a jagged, saw-tooth pattern that gradually falls below the horizontal line at 0.9. The confidence intervals show a jagged line that is above the horizontal line at 0.9. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
148 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1772 0 R/A 1771 0 R/Alt(The classical interval, H P D interval and equal tail credible interval are graphed. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures lambda and ranges from 0 to 1.5 in increments of 0.5. The vertical axis has a point labeled C equals 0.44.  A curve labeled pi, open parenthesis, lambda, the absolute value of sigma x, close parenthesis,  originating close to origin peaks at 0.6 and declines to \(1,0.44\) and \(1.5, 0\). The x-intercepts of C equals 0.44 of the graph are indicated and the distance between them are labeled H P D. The distance between 0.25 and 1.25 is labeled classical. The distance between 0.3 and 1.1 are labeled equal tail. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
149 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1774 0 R/A 1773 0 R/Alt(The asymptomatic relative efficiency is compared to the gamma mean. All curves have A R E values higher than 1. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures the gamma mean and ranges from 0 to 10 in increments of 2. The vertical axis measures the A R E and ranges from 0 to 8 in increments of 1. Four curves are depicted. A curve originates at \(1, 1.8\) and ends at \(1.2, 10\). A curve originates at \(1, 3.4\) and ends at \(1.4, 10\).  A curve originates at \(1.5, 5.2\), decreases to \(2, 3\) and ends at \(1.6, 10\). A curve originates at \(2, 8\), drops to \(2.2, 5\) and ends at \(2, 10\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
150 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1776 0 R/A 1775 0 R/Alt(A histogram with multiple bars between the horizontal axis values of 0 to 4 shows a declining trend. A curve overlying the edges of the histogram shows a concave, decreasing trend. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures negative 2 log with values ranging from 0 to 4 in increments of 0.5. The vertical axis measures p d f with values ranging from 0 to 3 in increments of 0.5. The first bar from the left is the highest and the last bar on the right is the lowest. A curve originating at \(0, 3\), drops to \(0.1, 1\), \(0.5, 0.5\), \(1.5, 0.2\) and \(4, 0\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
151 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1778 0 R/A 1777 0 R/Alt(The realized intervals of L R T, score, and modified Wald procedures. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures p and ranges from 0 to 1 in increments of 0.2. The vertical axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 12 in increments of 2. 13 sets of L R T, score, and modified Wald procedures are depicted. L R T procedures have shortest intervals and score procedures have the longest intervals.  \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
152 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1780 0 R/A 1779 0 R/Alt(The coverage probabilities are compared with P values in a graph. It shows the L R T procedure, score procedure, and Wald procedure. A horizontal line represents a coverage probability value of 0.9. \n\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
153 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1782 0 R/A 1781 0 R/Alt(The calculation of the vertical distance of data points from the line of best fit in a scatter plot using R S S.\n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 4 in increments of 1. The vertical axis measures y and ranges from 0 to 5 in increments of 1. 22 data points are plotted on the x y plane. A diagonal line labeled y equals c plus d x, originating at \(0,1.9\) passes through \(\(2,3\) and \(4, 4.5\) is as close as possible to all data points. The vertical distance from two data points to the diagonal line is indicated using vertical lines. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
154 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1784 0 R/A 1783 0 R/Alt(The geometric description of BLUE is two intersecting planes, labeled sigma d I equals 0 and sigma d I x I equals 1. A dotted sphere is indicated at the area of the intersection of the planes. An ellipse touching two points of the sphere is indicated in the sigma d I equals 0 plane.  Points 0 and d are labeled at the center of the ellipse and on the margin of the ellipse respectively. A line extends from the point 0 to d. \n\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
155 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1786 0 R/A 1785 0 R/Alt(An example of Scheffe band, two Bonferroni intervals and a t interval.\n\n"\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 4 in increments of 1. The vertical axis measures y and ranges from 0 to 6 in increments of 1. The data points are plotted between 1 and 5 of y axis and 0 and 4 of x-axis. A diagonal line originating at \(0,1.9\), increases toward the right to reach \(4, 4\). A dotted line above the diagonal line follows an increasing a concave, increasing trend. A dotted line below the diagonal line follows an increasing, convex increasing trend. Three error bars are indicated between the dotted lines at x-axis values of 1, 3 and 3.5. All data are approximate. \n"\n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
156 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1788 0 R/A 1787 0 R/Alt(The orthogonal least square distance and ordinary least square distance of a data point from the line of best fit.\n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 5 in increments of 1. The vertical axis measures y and ranges from 0 to 5 in increments of 1. A diagonal line originating at \(0,1\), increases toward the right to reach \(4, 3\).  Two data points \(x hat, y hat\) and \(x prime, y prime\) are indicated at \(2.5, 2\) and \(1.8, 4.1\). The distance between the two data points is indicate using a vertical line labeled ordinary least square distance. The distance between the line of best fit and the point \(x prime, y prime\) is indicated using a straight line and labeled orthogonal least squares distance. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
157 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1790 0 R/A 1789 0 R/Alt(A scatter plot with multiple data points and three lines of regression, orthogonal regression, regression of x on y, and regression of y on x.\n\nThe horizontal axis measures x and ranges from 0 to 4 in increments of 1. The vertical axis measures y and ranges from 0 to 5 in increments of 1. The data points are scattered between x values of 0 and 3 and y values of 0 and 5. The orthogonal regression line, y equals negative .49 plus 1.88 x extends from \(0.4, 0\) to \(3,5\). The regression of x on y, y equals negative 2.31 plus 2.82 x extends from \(0.8, 0\) to \(2.5, 5\). The regression of y on x, y equals 1.86 plus 0.68 x extends from \(0, 1.9\) to \(4, 4.4\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
158 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1792 0 R/A 1791 0 R/Alt(The F statistic function for a set of data is depicted. The graph depicts a curve with two peaks and two horizontal lines representing, F subscript 1, 22, 0.01, and F subscript 1, 22, 0.05. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis measures beta and ranges from negative 20 to 10 in increments of 5. The vertical axis measures the F statistic and ranges from 0 to 10 in increments of 5. Two horizontal lines labeled F subscript 1, 22, 0.05 and F subscript 1, 22, 0.01 extend from points \(0, 4.2\) and \(0, 8\), toward the right. A curve originative at \(0, 8\), increases to reach \(negative 2, 10\) and drops to \(negative 1, 0\) which is indicated as negative 1 over beta. It increases to \(1, 10\) and drops to \(2,0\) which is indicated as beta. It increases to reach \(10, 5\). All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
159 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1794 0 R/A 1793 0 R/Alt(The temperature at flight time and failure of O rings, along with a fitted curve. \n\nIn the graph, the horizontal axis ranges from 0 to 85 in increments of 5. The vertical axis ranges from 0 to 1 in increments of 0.2. Six data points are along the y axis values of 1 and 12 data points are at x-axis values of 0. A concave down, decreasing curve originating at \(0,1\), drops to \(67, 0.4\) and inflects to follow a concave up, decreasing trend to reach \(0, 81\) and trails away. All data are approximate. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
160 0 obj<</K 0/Pg 1796 0 R/A 1795 0 R/Alt(The least squares, L A D, and M-estimate best fits for two sets of data, that is, original and modified data. The L A D and M estimates show no noticeable change for the original and modified data; however, the least squares best fit reflects the change. \n)/T()/P 27 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj
